;;; May 11, 1988   	1)  Converted the debugger to run;	in ROM.  On power-up the debugger;	looks like a DA and then the system;	copies itself into RAM (entire 64K bank);	It then steals the DA vector and then;	replaces the starting address of the debugger;	in its place.  Once the debugger has been invoked,;	it asks for 1K of bank zero memory for stack and;	direct page use.  It saves the environment of any;	memory that it uses.  It presently uses the DA ;	calls to save and restore the text screens for exit;;	2)  Cleaned up several bugs that had been present ;	from the beginning of time:;;	A)  The debugger screwed up the stack when it;	transferred from native mode to emulation mode;	and back.  I saved the upper half of the native;	mode stack pointer and restore it when going;	to native.;;	B)  When loading files to debug, the system loader;	sets aside a portion of zero page to hold some;	variables it needs.  When the debugger quit;	it did not dispose of this memory.  This made;	it impossible for ProDOS 8 applications to;	run following the debugger because they reserve;	all of bank zero.  I saved all of the file ID's;	and then dispose of them and all of their memory;	when I quit.  This also enabled people to;	load more than one file at a time and still;	keep track of all the memory.  15 max files;	can be loaded.;;	3)  Added the feature to allow JSR's to be run real;	time.  Before only JSL's could.  Now this only;	works in RAM.;;	4)  Fixed the re-entrancy problem.  Once you are inside;	the debugger, attempts to access it again are useless.;	The only way to re-enter is to quit and then get;	back in using CTRL-OA-OPTION-ESC.;; May 22,1988	1)  Stored the current stack in 01/0100 on exit.  To keep;	interrupts from trashing my input/output variables,;	I pushed them on entrant stack and then saved stack;	in 01/0100.  Problem was that stack was drifting down;	every time the debugger was called about 1B bytes and;	quickly drifted into zero page and died!!.  I'm now;	saving the stack on exit.  Now the stack drifts three;	bytes every time (from rtl that follows).  Will check and;	see if that should be fixed.;;	2)  saved and restored the graphics switches -- including;	the super hi-res switch.  Now you can return to the finder;	and see the pretty screen.  Only works if you have not;	used the graphics screens -- I'm still thinking about ;	that!!;; May 23,1988	1)  When a break occurred, the break handler was still set;	to the ROM bank.  This was changed to be in the RAM bank.;;	2)  When the jump (J) command was used, the proper bank was;	not saved in MYID to be used to get the proper address;	to jump to like it is in the JSL and JSR.  This was fixed.;;	3)  A problem was occurring with the heart beat task.  If the;	DA vector had not changed from the last task and was;	still my vector, then the task replaced the CDA vector;	with my vector.  CDA's were never available and trying;	to get at one hung the system in a wait loop.;;	4)  Added three more to the stack before restoring it in QUIT.;	This insures that the stack pointer is in the EXACT same;	place as when it entered the debugger.  Should make ;	everyone happy.!!;;	5)  The stack is still causing me problems.  This time I'm;	saving the emulation stack pointer away and then restoring;	it when I leave.  This way it is always exactly the same.;; May 26,1988	1)  Fixed subtle stack problem.  The lower byte of the A reg;	wasn't being saved in the save loop due to a bne that;	should have been a bpl.  If the debugger was called;	in between an instruction that loaded A and then ;	transferred it to the stack, the stack got trashed because;	the proper value wasn't being restored.;; May 27,1988	1)  Added the code that allows real time JSR's to be run;	out of ROM!! The code keeps a table for each ROM bank;	The first time a ROM JSR is called, the code searches the;	bank for any RTL and uses that address for the RTS to;	return to.  The next time a JSR is needed in the same;	bank, it has it saved and doesn't need to look for it.;	Now there is no provision for what happens if there is;	no RTL anywhere in the bank.;; May 31,1988	1)  Went through and added all of the longa and longi's to;	go with the rep's and sep's in the code.  Did it care-;	fully to avoid any crashes when the code is assembled;	and run.;; June 1,1988	1)  Made a special ROM for the people who changed the CDA ;	vector.  The normal one is E1/0048, their's is E1/02B4;; June 2,1988	1)  One good hack deserves another.  For Fern's special ROM's;	I set up the CDA vector to point to the manager -- like it;	supposedly is on power-up.  This insures that you can ;	always get into the DA's;;	2)  Changed the save and restore screen calls from the DA one;	to the one that requests a handle and then puts all of the;	memory there.  There is no provision for error handling yet.  ;	This allows DA's to be called from the debugger.;; June 8,1988	1)  There is a problem with the boot code turning off the ;	VBL's. This caused problems because the heartbeat task ;	needed to keep me installed was not being called.  Thus, ;	the hack:  when the debugger is called, I re-install my ;	heartbeat task.  If it is already there, I simply turn on ;	VBL's and go for it.  If it is not there, then I assume ;	that a pr#5 or something similar wiped me out of the ;	heartbeat queue.  I install myself, clear out my queue and ;	turn on VBL's.  With this solution, the worst thing that ;	can happen, is that someone will try to get into DA's, find;	they can't, access the DA's.;; June 8,1988	1)  Changed the base conversion feature to handle expressions of ;	32-bit numbers. An expression consists of a series of one or;	more hex or decimal numbers separated by operators and ;	ending with an equal sign. Hex numbers must be preceded by ;	a '$'.  Decimal numbers may be preceded by a positive or;	negative sign or nothing.  Negative numbers are converted ;	to 32-bit two's-complement.  Numbers that are larger that ;	32-bits will wrap. Legal operators are '+', '-','*', '/', ;	and '%' (modulus).  All operators are evaluated left to ;	right with equal precedence. Overflow is ignored. The result ;	will be printed in either hex or decimal depending on the ;	form of the expression. If the expression consists of just one ;	hex number, then it is assumed we want to convert it to ;	decimal, so the result will be in decimal.  If the expression;	is just one decimal number, the result is printed in hex. ;	If the expression contains more than one number and all the ;	numbers are in decimal,then the result is printed in decimal.  ;	Otherwise the result is printed in hex.;;	2)  Changed command parser to read command starting at the ;	rightmost equal sign in the current line that is not at the ;	end of the line.  This allows the result of a calculation to ;	be used in a command without having to retype the result.;; June 9, 1988	1)  Added indirect memory referencing.  An address followed by two ;	colons will dump memory starting at the 3-byte address which ;	is stored at the address given in the command.;	;	2)  Added direct page mode for the memory subdisplay.  Typing 'Z' ;	while editing a memory cell will toggle direct page mode.  ;	A direct page memory cell will display the given direct page ;	address even when the direct page changes.;; June 10, 1988	1)  Added a new disassembler mode that causes the disassembler to;	recognize REP's and SEP's and switches between long and short ;	accumulator and index registers automatically during ;	disassembly.The mode is controlled by the disassembly mode ;	flag (d).  If d is set, then SEP's and REP's are recognized; ;	if d is clear, then they are ignored.  The flag can be set or ;	cleared by the command d=x, where x is either 0 or 1.;; June 13, 1988	1)  Added dereferencing mode for the memory subdisplay.  Typing ;	'I' while editing a memory cell will toggle deref mode.;	Two colons are used to indicate a deref.  The memory ;	displayed will be the contents of the memory location whose;	address is stored in the three bytes starting at the address;	of the memory cell.;; June 14, 1988	1)  Added alignment of bytes in the dump display, so that the ;	position of the byte is determined by the low nibble of it's ;	address.;;	2)  Added a dump range option.  The command 'addr1.addr2:' dumps;	memory between and including addr1 and addr2.  If the number;	of bytes requested is greater than the number that will fit ;	on the screen, then only what will fit on the screen is ;	displayed.;; June 15, 1988	1)  The deref memory dump option was extended so that the number;	of colons following the dump address or range is the number of;	bytes in the indirect address.  That is the command of the ;	form '$XXXXXX::' will dump the block of memory starting at ;	the two byte address stored at $XXXXXX.  The bank is decided;	by the value of the bank register.  A command of the form ;	'$XXXXXX:::' will dump the block of memory starting at the ;	three byte address stored at $XXXXXX.;;	2)  The memory subdisplay was updated to handle both 16-bit and;	24-bit dereferencing.  The referencing is done as described ;	for the dumping. The type of referencing (16-bit, 24-bit, or ;	none) is changed by typing a colon while editing a memory ;	cell.  The current referencing mode is indicated by the ;	number of colons following the address as described above.;;	3)  Allowed entering of addresses into memory.  When setting;	memory, if the value is longer than 1 byte, it is assumed ;	to be an address. For example, typing '00/2000:123456' will;	store a $56 at 00/2000, a $34 at 00/2001, and a $12 at ;	00/2002.  Note that if the address is a 24-bit address, ;	you must explicitly enter leading zero's.If you want to ;	store the 24-bit address 00/1234 at 00/2000, you must type ;	'00/2000:001234'.  If you type '00/2000:1234', it will be ;	assumed to be a 16-bit address, and only two bytes will be ;	entered into memory.  Note that the v1.0d1 ERS (5/11/88) ;	implies that this feature already exists.  In fact, ;	it did not exist.;;	4)  Added a quickdump feature.  If you type an address and then;	press return without following it with a colon, then the 16 ;	bytes starting at that address are displayed at the bottom ;	of the screen.  They will stay there until any key is pressed.;	This keypress will be passed to the input routine, so it will ;	be the first character of the next command.;; June 16, 1988	1)  Changed the expression routines so that decimal results are ;	preceded by a plus.  This makes sure that they are not ;	confused with hex results.;;	2)  Added a column heading line to the dump display.;; June 20, 1988	1)  A circular history queue was added to record the last 32 ;	instructions in a trace.  This queue is used to show the ;	trace history when returning from tracing with the display;	off. This means that a fast trace can be done by turning ;	the display off first, but that you can still see the ;	instructions that have been executed when done tracing.;; June 21, 1988	1)  Added display mode switches to the command mode.  These ;	switches are similar to the ones available in trace mode.  ;	The backslash key '\' is now treated specially.  If this ;	key is pressed any time while on the command line, then ;	the next key pressed will be interpreted as a display switch;	key.  The display switch keys are the same as in trace;	mode.  Neither the backslash or the switch key will be ;	displayed, so they can be entered in the middle of a command;	without affecting it.;; June 22, 1988	1)  Initial register values are set to the saved irq values ;	found in the $E1000 bank.;; June 23, 1988	1)  Removed previous change (hopefully temporarily).;; June 27, 1988	1)  Fixed trace history to work with stepping and with tracing;	using the 'T' command without an address.;; June 28, 1988	1)  Fixed 80COLSTORE switch bug.  The debugger now restores the;	value of the switch after it clears it.;; June 30, 1988	1)  Added conditional assembly stuff to allow the creation of;	an init version of the debugger.  This version should be;	made into a file type of $B6 on the GS.  It will load on;	bootup and act exactly like the rom version.;;	2)  Updated the copyright/version message.;; July 5, 1988	1)  Removed code that initialized the display screen (i.e.;	break points, mem protection, mem cells, stack display,;	etc.)  The initial values are now determined by the;	values in the various tables and variables at assembly;	time.  Doing this means that if the values are changed;	when in the desk accessory version, then if we exit the;	debugger and then return, the values will be the same.;;	2)  Fixed bug in quick dump routine that caused crash when;	exiting the debugger.;;	3)  Modified code so that the entry state is now saved as;	variables instead of on the stack.;;	4)  No longer return to calling procedure with a rtl.  Now;	we pull the return address off of the stack on entry;	and do a jmp to that address to exit.;;	5)  The return address and processor status that were saved;	on the user's stack by the interrupt routines are now;	saved on entry to the debugger, and then restored on;	exit.  This allows the user to trace his/her program;	in the debugger without having to worry about destroying;	the return stuff on the stack.;; July 6, 1988	1)  On initial startup, the break vector is patched to jump;	into the debugger.  When a break in encountered, the;	the debugger will launch and check the break status flag.;	If it is set to debugger breaks, then the debugger will;	beep and then set all of the registers to reflect the;	status of the machine at the time of the break.  The stack;	pointer will be set to it's value at the time of the break;	(not after the break,) and the PC will be set to the;	address of the break instruction.  If the break status;	flag is set to use breaks, then the the debugger will just;	break to the monitor.;;	2)  When entering the debugger by using the entry key;	sequence, debugger will reflect the state of the machine;	when the interrupt occurred.;;	3)  Fixed bug that caused crash when doing a J or an X with;	either m=1 or e=1.  The fix involved restoring some lines;	that had been commented out in the jump routine (JPC.);;	4)  Added new debugger command 'START' that prints out the;	address at which the debugger is loaded.  This is to;	help in the debugging of the debugger.  This command is;	only available if the debugger is assembled with;	ThisIsDEV equal to 1.;; July 7, 1988	1)  Fixed bug in the routine that check to see if the break;	vector has already been patched.  It now compares the;	bank in the break vector against the current bank, not;	the bank at assembly (or load) time.  This was only a;	problem in the rom version.;; July 8, 1988	1)  Fixed bug that that caused problems when executing an JSL;	or JSR at real time with the emulation mode on.;; 	2)  Fixed bug that caused the high byte of both X and Y to be;	trashed when returning from executing a JSL or JSR at real;	time.;;	3)  Fixed bug in 'X' command that sometimes caused a jump to;	the wrong bank.;;	4)  Fixed bug that caused a crash when tracing a JSR in real;	time with short accumulator.;;	5)  Added two new commands: STACK and DP.  These commands;	respectively set the users stack pointer and direct page;	pointer to point to the stack and direct page that are;	allocated by the debugger on startup.  These are primarily;	useful for when the user enters the non-application version;	of the debugger, and doesn't want a safe stack and direct;	page to use instead of using those of the application that;	was running at the time that the debugger was called.  Note;	that the stack command only works if e = 0.;;	6)  Fixed bug that caused the stack pointer to be off by one;	when returning from real time code in emulation mode via;	a BRK instruction.];; July 11, 1988	1)  Fixed bug in the loops that saved and restored the entry;	state.;; July 12, 1988	1)  Fixed bug that caused the bank byte of the break vector;	to be patched incorrectly in the rom version.;;	2)  Fixed bug that caused problems with real breakpoints;; July 13, 1988	1)  Fixed bug that caused real breakpoints to mess up on the;	first J or X command executed after a trace or single;	step with the display on.;; July 15, 1988	1)  If the option key is held down when the debugger is;	first called during initialization, the debugger will;	not be installed.;;	2)  Fixed bug that caused the debugger's memory not to be;	deallocated by the QUIT command in the init version.;;	3)  Fixed bug that caused PC not to be set when entering;	debugger via a break.;; July 18, 1988	1)  Moved allocation of 1k back to every time debugger is;	run.;;	2)  Fixed bug that caused the PCK on entry not to be;	restored on exit if in emulation mode.;;	3)  Debugger now recognizes CTRL-OA-Option-ESC while doing;	real-time execution within the debugger.  This will not;	re-enter the debugger recursively, but will just stop;	execution of the code and drop you back to the main;	debugger display screen.  When quitting, you will still;	exit to the point at which the debugger was first called.;;	4)  The Prodos 16 stack based system call is now recognized,;	and all JSLs to it are executed at real time.;;	5)  The PC was being set to two bytes past the break when;	entering the debugger via a brk instruction.  Now it;	is set to point to the brk instruction.;; July 19, 1988	1)  When doing the QUIT command, the debugger checks if the;	user has patched the break vector since the debugger;	patched it since initialization.  If not, then it is;	restored to what it was before initialization.;;	2)  Version # is now followed by a letter indicating which;	version of the debugger.  The letters are 'r' for the;	rom version, 'i' for the init version, and 'a' for the;	application version.;; July 21, 1988	1)  Changed user breaks to monitor breaks.  This just means;	that the command to set breaks to drop into the monitor;	is now 'mbrk' instead of 'ubrk.'  Also the flag that;	indicates the type of break is now an 'm' instead of a 'u'.;;	2)  Changed commands around to remove almost all case;	sensitivity.  To do this several things were changed.;	First of all, the commands 'e=', 'm=', and 'x=' were;	removed.  These can still be changed by toggling them with;	the 'e', 'm', and 'x' commands, which must be in lower;	case.  The old 'M=' and 'X=' commands now work in lower;	case as well as upper case.  The old command 'D' which;	was not case sensitive has been changed to 'DP:'.  This;	is so that the old command 'd=' could be removed and;	replaced with the new command 'd' which toggles the;	disassembly mode.  The old command 'DP' was changed to;	'DPAGE'.  This was done so as to avoid typing it;	accidentally when meaning to type 'DP:'.  Finally, the;	'quit' command was changed to 'unload'.;;	3)  Fixed bug that caused error when doing a command of the;	form '1234X' or '1234J' from the dump screen.;;	4)  The command parser now only starts interpreting the ;	currentcommand from the previous equal sign if that ;	equal sign was from a valid expression.  This eliminates;	some confusion from occurring when trying to set an ;	invalid flag equal to a value.;;	5)  Fixed bug that caused numbers of more than 3 bytes that ;	were entered on the command line to be truncated to 2 ;	bytes.;;	6)  The default base for numbers not preceded by any sign;	in an expression is now hex.  Decimal numbers now must;	alway be preceded by a positive or negative sign.;	This means that if you want to add hexadecimal $1A to;	decimal 5 for example, you must now type '1A++5='.;;	7)  The 'J' command (without an address in front of it);	was not working corectly.  The code was doing some strange;	things that didn't seem to make much sense.  I added code;	that should make it do the right thing.  Let's hope it;	works OK.;;	8)  Made the letter 'G' interchangable with the letter 'J';	for jumping into code.  This was done because everyone;	(myself) included thought that there already was a 'G';	command, but it didn't exist.;; July 22, 1988	1)  Changed the 'on' command so that it always causes the;	master display screen to be displayed.  It used to ignore;	the on command the screen was on, but we were viewing;	a different screen by using the backslash commands.;; July 25, 1988	1)  Took out fix that stopped numbers of greater than 3 bytes;	to be truncated.  It was causing too many problems.;;	2)  Fixed bug that caused CLOAD and CSAVE not to work in;	ROM version.;;	3)  Fixed bug with the automatic switching back to the;	master display screen when typing a command that;	isn't available from the dump screen.;;	4)  Fixed bug that caused an expression of the form;	'5A=' not to be converted to decimal.;; July 26, 1988	1)  Fixed bug that caused master display screen to be turned;	back on automatically if doing a trace or single-step;	while it was turned off.;; July 27, 1988	1)  Took out the letters following the version number.;; July 28, 1988	1)  Corrected the help screens.  Made changes so that there;	are no invalid commands, but didn't add all the new;	commands.;; Aug. 1, 1988	1)  Toolbox calls are now recognized during disassembly.;	If a JSL $E10000 is seen, then the debugger will check;	if the previous instruction was a LDX immediate and if;	long indexing is on.  If both of these are true, then;	the tool call name will be displayed instead of the;	JSL E10000.  The LDX # will still be displayed.;;	2)  It is now possible to return from the monitor (after;	entering it via the MON command) by pressing;	CTRL-OPTION-OA-ESC.;; Aug. 3, 1988	1)  Fixed bug that caused the debugger to be messed up after;	hitting a BRK or pressing CTRL-OA-OPTION-ESC while;	tracing a subroutine in real-time.;;	2)  Fixed bug/feature that pushed an extra long return;	address onto the stack when executing a JSR in real;	time.;; Aug. 5, 1988	1)  Fixed another bug that caused the debugger to crash on;	exit if a BRK was hit or CTRL-OPTION-OA-ESC was pressed;	while inside a real time JSR.;;	2)  Debugger now restores the next instruction if a BRK was;	encountered or if CTRL-OA-OPTION-ESC wass pressed while;	tracing a subroutine in real time.;;	3)  The quit command now quits to the current PC and ;	current state instead of to the point of entry.  The;	screens are still set to what they were on entry.;; Aug. 10,1988	1)  Fixed bug that caused the high byte of both X and Y to;	be destroyed when entering a subroutin in real time.;;	2)  Added an unload by user ID command.  This command is;	of the form 'UNLOAD id#' and simply calls the;	tool box routine DisposeAll with the  given ID #.;;	3)  The routine that checks for toolbox calls during;	disassembly now no longer checks to make sure that;	long indexing is on.  This was causing problems when;	code was being entered with the mini-assembler.;;	4)  Added two new commands, 'TOOL #toolnum' and;	'TOOL _ToolName'  Both of these commands output the;	tool number and name of the specified tool.  If;	an unknown tool number is requested, then the tool;	name will be output as unknown.  If an unknown tool;	name is requested, the debugger will just beep.;; Aug. 11, 1988	1)  Changed the UNLOAD command to SHUTDOWN.  It now takes;	two arguments, the user ID and a quit flag, and executes;	a UserShutDown call.  The command should be of the form;	'SHUTDOWN id# quit_flag.';;	2)  The TOOL command now also prints the actual address;	of the tool routine.;;	3)  Pressing 'S' while in set mode now toggles the stack;	subdisplay between showing stack addresses and stack;	offsets.;;	4)  Pressing 'D' while in set mode now toggles the dump;	alignment on and off.  If dump alignment is on, then;	memory dumps will be aligned to 16-byte boundaries.;; Aug. 12, 1988	1)  Fixed bug that caused extra bytes to be left on the;	stack when returning from a real time JSR.;;	2)  Fixed bug that caused major problems when entering real;	time JSR's and JSL's in emulation mode.;;	3)  The debugger now recognizes GS/OS calls (JSL $E100A8);	and formats them in disassemblies.;; Aug. 18, 1988	1)  Added the ability to load and use templates.  A file of;	templates can be loaded in with the command:;	'LOADTEMP TemplateFile'.  An error $FFFF while loading;	a template file means that the file is not in the correct;	format. A template can be used with;	the command '_TemplateName address' where address is;	the address of your data.  A template file consists;	of one or more templates.  Each template should start;	with a line of the form '_START TemplateName' and end;	with a line '_END'.  Anything not between a start and;	end will be ignored.  Each line in the template should;	be of the form 'Label type count'.  The fields should be;	seperated by on or more tabs or spaces.  The count field;	can be omited signifying just one value.  Types may;	be either 'byte', 'word', 'long', 'ascii', 'cstring',;	or 'pstring' and may be abreviated to their first letter.;	All templates can be unloaded by the command 'KILLTEMP'.;; Feb. 2, 1989	1)  The user ID used for the stack and direct page segment;	was being read of real zero page.  This random value sometimes; 	worked and sometimes did not.;; Feb. 16, 1989	1)  Debugger now loads in a default configuration file on;	startup.  This file should be called 'DEBUG.SETUP'.  It;	should be located in the same folder as the debugger for;	the init version, and should be located in the;	SYSTEM/SYSTEM.SETUP folder of the bootup disk for the init;	and ROM versions.;;	2)  If breakpoints are in when the BP command is entered, they;	will automatically be taken out before the user edits the;	breakpoints.  When the user is done editing the breakpoints,;	they will automatically be put back in.;; Feb. 22, 1989	1)  Now check to see if the printer slot is selected before;	allowing the screen to be printed.  Also make sure that slot;	three is selected before allowing the MON command.  These;	changes will supposedly prevent bugs in system 5.0.;; Feb. 22, 1989	1)  Fixed file so that tabs are set to 20.  It used to be;	that everything was indented with spaces and some tabs.;	This made it a real pain to edit the code.;; Feb. 23, 1989	1)  Added a scroll down (SCROLLDN) and a scroll dissasembly;	subdisplay down routines.  These are to be used with viewing;	history.;; Feb. 24, 1989	1)  Found and fixed three SEP #30's and two REP #30's.;	These should have been SEP #$30 and REP #$30.  This fixed;	the ten-speed bug and who knows what else.;; Mar. 3, 1989	1)  Changed quit command to QR (for quit resume) and put;	back the old quit command as Q (or QUIT).  The Q command;	restores the state of the machine to what it was on entry.;;	2)  Default config file now called GSBUG.SETUP not;	DEBUG.SETUP.;;	3)  We no longer try to load a default config file in the;	ROM version.;;	4)  No longer define ThisIsRAM in this file.  It must be;	defined on the command line.;; Mar. 10, 1989	1)  Fixed bug that caused stack to be messed up when;	tracing in emulation mode.  Now all of the save and;	restore register routines are macros.  Before they;	were copies of the same code.;;	2)  Debugger stack is now allocated as not locked;	and is then locked afterwards.  This causes it to be;	allocated near the top of bank 0, and not at $2000.;	This fixes the bug that caused crashes when trying to;	view page 2.;;	3)  Quit command no longer works from init or rom versions.;	the RESUME command (R or RESUME) does what QR was doing.;	UNLOAD unloads the debugger and then does a resume.  There;	is no command that does what quit did.;;	4)  Added two new commands: SE (Save Environment) and;	RE (Restore Environment).  SE saves a snapshot of the;	current state of the registers.  RE will restore this;	saved state.  If the RE command is used when no state;	has been saved with the SE command, then the state of;	the registers on entry will be restored.;; Mar. 16, 1989	1)  Fixed bug that caused set command to no longer work.;; Mar. 17, 1989	1)  Added saved trace history.  You can set trace save ranges;	in the MP subdisplay.  The H command sets a window.  If;	any windows are set, then only instructions inside of;	the window are saved in the trace.  Open a trace history;	file with the command 'OPENHIST filename'.  All instructions;	in the trace history range will be saved to the file allong;	with the current state of the registers.  Close the history;	trace file with the command 'CLOSEHIST'.  View a history;	trace with the command 'VIEWHIST filename'.  When viewing ;	history, the current instruction is highlighted and the;	registers reflect the state of the registers just before that;	instruction was executed.  Use the up and down arrows to move;	up and down one step.  Use the arrows with OA to move a;	page at a time.  Use the left and right arrows to move to;	the start or end.  Type a step number to move to that step.;	Press esc to finish viewing the history.;;	2)  Added breaks on toolcalls and toolcall errors in trace mode.;	To set a break on a toolcall use the command 'SetTBrk #toolnum';	or 'SetTBrk _toolname'.  To clear a break on a toolcall, use;	the command 'ClrTBrk' followed by the name or number.  To clear;	all breaks on a toolcall, use 'ClrAllTBrk'.  If a break is set,;	execution will halt on the toolcall, before it is executed.  The;	commands to set and clear breaks on tool errors are 'SetEBrk',;	'ClrEBrk', and ClrAllEBrk'.  These will cause the trace to halt;	if the given toolcall returns an error.  The debugger will halt;	on the next instruction without executing it.  If either byte of;	the tool call number equals zero, then that byte will match any;	high byte.  For example, setting a break on $0004 will cause a;	break on any Quick Draw call, while $0000 will cause a break on;	any call.  Up to twenty of each kind of break can be set.  The;	set breaks will be saved with your configuration.  To view the;	currently set breaks, use the command 'ShowBrks'.;;	1)  Fixed expresions, hex #'s weren't right.;;	2)  Fixed check for $C017 before entry into mon.;; March 20, 1989	1)  Can now break on tool calls and tool call errors while;	in real time.  This can be done by using the commands;	TBRKIN and EBRKIN to set real time tool breaks and real time;	tool error breaks respectively.  To disable real time;	breaks, use the commands TBRKOUT and EBRKOUT.;;	2)  Fixed problem that caused the PC to incorectly reflect;	the current instruction when halting from a trace while the;	display is off.;